WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.320
For years, React Native relied on an asynchronous bridge to pass messages.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.480
Developers wrote the glue code by hand to connect these two realms.

3
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.040
Because the data was serialized and sent over at runtime,

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.960
there was no way to guarantee the shapes matched.

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.400
A typo in JavaScript could send a string instead of a number,

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.200
resulting in a silent failure or a hard crash on the native side.

7
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.840
The new architecture replaces this fragile runtime messaging with rigid native interfaces.

8
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.840
Code Gen operates exclusively as a build time compiler to achieve this stability.

9
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.520
It reads a strictly typed TypeScript specification file.

10
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.560
From that specification, it automatically generates abstract classes,

11
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.280
protocols, and JSI dispatch tables.

12
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.720
This is the native boilerplate that historically caused errors.

13
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.760
Now you never write the C++ or Java bridging glue by hand.

14
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.880
The TypeScript file becomes the absolute single source of truth

15
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.240
for the entire cross-platform architecture.

16
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.200
By declaring a module's interface in TypeScript,

17
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.080
you are physically binding the underlying native implementations to that exact shape.

18
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.200
This shift transforms TypeStafety into a compile-time requirement,

19
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.000
preventing mismatches before the code ever reaches a device.

20
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.480
To see how this model executes in practice,

21
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.560
we start at the exact entry point of the configuration, the package.json file.

22
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.240
Code Gen requires explicit mapping instructions

23
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.000
to translate JavaScript declarations into native build targets.

24
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.600
The name configuration property defines the exact CMake target name for Android,

25
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.600
as well as the framework name for iOS when generating the spec group.

26
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.120
Next, the compiler needs to know where the source files are located,

27
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.680
defined by the source directory property.

28
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.720
Code Gen specifically scans this path looking for files that match the native.ts naming convention.

29
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.560
For the Android routing configuration, you provide a Java package name.

30
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.600
This dictates the exact name space where the generated Android interface classes will reside.

31
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.400
The iOS equivalent is the module's provider property.

32
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.760
This mapping explicitly dictates how a given JavaScript module string

33
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.440
corresponds to the specific native class that the system needs to instantiate.

34
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.520
This JSON configuration acts as a strict routing table.

35
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.960
It ensures the build-time compiler knows exactly where the source of truth lives

36
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.480
and precisely where the compiled outputs must be delivered.

37
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:28.680
When we follow an input file like nativecalculator.ts through this pipeline into the Android environment,

38
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.560
we see the architectural enforcement in action.

39
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.440
Code Gen produces a specific file for the Android build called nativecalculatorspec.java.

40
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.000
Developers must never manually edit this generated spec file.

41
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.560
It is entirely controlled by the compiler.

42
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.800
The developer's sole responsibility on the native side is writing the actual implementation logic.

43
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:55.760
You write a custom Java or Kotlin class, but it is strictly required to use the extends keyword,

44
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.040
targeting the generated spec.

45
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:04.520
By acting as an abstract class, the generated spec dictates a rigid mathematical contract.

46
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:10.240
This inheritance pattern forces a strict validation during the local Android build process.

47
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.120
If your native implementation is missing a method that you defined in the TypeScript spec,

48
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.920
the Java compiler will immediately throw a fatal error and block the build.

49
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.520
This hardware-level validation eliminates the possibility of a JavaScript function

50
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.640
attempting to call an Android method that doesn't exist at runtime.

51
00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.880
Code Gen weaponizes the local Java compiler to enforce the front-end TypeScript contract

52
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.960
long before the application is ever deployed.

53
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.560
The exact same TypeScript specification feeds a parallel compilation pipeline for iOS.

54
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.520
Here, code Gen produces a header file called nativecalculatorspec.h.

55
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:54.040
This header file defines an objective C++ protocol that dictates the required interface for Apple devices.

56
00:03:54.040 --> 00:04:00.160
When you write your custom iOS implementation class, you must explicitly adopt this generated protocol.

57
00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:07.000
Just like the abstract class in Android, this protocol forces the native code to match the TypeScript definition perfectly.

58
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.720
But there is a strict requirement for your iOS implementation file.

59
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.360
It must end with the .mm extension.

60
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.600
A standard objective C file, ending .m, will completely fail in this architecture.

61
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:25.240
This is because turbo modules communicate using the JavaScript interface, or JSI, which is written in C++.

62
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.400
Using .mm denotes objective C++, and it is absolutely mandatory.

63
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:36.120
It is the only way to successfully bridge standard iOS code with the underlying JSI dispatch tables.

64
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.200
On iOS, code Gen binds front-end TypeScript types to the JSI dispatch tables,

65
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.000
ensuring native execution remains in lockstep with the JavaScript thread.

66
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.040
A common developer concern with build-time compilation is the assumed time cost of running a full X code or Gradle build,

67
00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.280
just to verify Type definitions.

68
00:04:54.280 --> 00:05:01.040
However, code Gen can be triggered from the terminal completely independently of the heavy iOS or Android app builds.

69
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.160
This isolated execution allows engineers to rapidly preview generated native specs

70
00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.680
and iterate on their TypeScript interfaces in milliseconds, without waiting for a full compile cycle.

71
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.600
Taking a step back, we can see the entire pipeline.

72
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.800
A single TypeScript file passes through the configuration backing into the compiler,

73
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:25.160
splitting into strict Java and Objective C++ specifications, which then lock into the custom implementations.

74
00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.080
This framework utilizes TypeScript as an infrastructure definition language,

75
00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.360
providing the rigid schema that governs both the JavaScript and native environments.

76
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.000
By standardizing the JSI dispatch tables at compile time,

77
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.600
the framework concretely eliminates the need to manually parse and evaluate JSON strings across a bridge.

78
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:49.080
Every method call is direct and prevalidated, removing the overhead and the risk inherent in the legacy system.

79
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.640
The ultimate operational benefit is a drastic reduction in production runtime crashes,

80
00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.280
caused by tight mismatches between the realms.

81
00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.680
With these native modules perfectly typed and safely compiled,

82
00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.680
the framework has the structural integrity required to optimize when they are loaded into memory.

83
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.040
This brings us to the next architectural phase,

84
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.080
utilizing these typed interfaces for defensive loading,

85
00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.840
a strategy designed to achieve near-instant application startup times.

